INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S QUARTERS - NIGHT

A round room papered in sea charts. A coal stove, a kettle, a narrow bed. The window shakes with wind. Every few seconds the lighthouse beam sweeps across the walls, white, then gone.

OLIVER FENWICK (50s, keeper, cardigan, pipe) pours tea. ESTHER FENWICK (50s, his wife, knitting) sits by the stove. Between them, their grown son TIMOTHY (20s, home from the mainland, restless) paces to the window and back.

OLIVER
Tea, Tim?

TIMOTHY
No, Dad.

ESTHER
He's nervous.

TIMOTHY
I'm not nervous.

ESTHER
You're wearing a groove in the floor.

OLIVER
Sit.

TIMOTHY
There's someone on the rocks.

OLIVER
There's no one on the rocks.

ESTHER ^
There's no one on the rocks.

TIMOTHY
I saw a light.

OLIVER
Our light.

TIMOTHY
No. Lower.

The beam sweeps. Passes. Then, from the dark below, a KNOCK. Three slow raps on the door.

Esther's needles stop. Oliver's pipe stops halfway to his mouth.

ESTHER
Who would be out in this?

Timothy opens the door. Wind whips in. On the step stands MARGUERITE HOLLOWAY (30s, drenched oilskin, hair plastered, lantern in hand, eyes calm).

MARGUERITE
Forgive the hour. My boat has run aground.

OLIVER
Come in, come in! Esther, a towel!

ESTHER
There's no boat on that shoal, Oliver. There hasn't been one since--

OLIVER
Esther.

Marguerite steps inside, drips onto the mat, offers a small smile.

MARGUERITE
A cup of tea would be kind.

TIMOTHY
Where did you moor?

MARGUERITE
Where the water let me.

ESTHER
That's not an answer.

OLIVER
She's half frozen!

TIMOTHY
Dad--

OLIVER
Tim!

ESTHER
Child, you are shaking.

MARGUERITE
Am I?

She looks at her hand, holding the lantern. Perfectly steady. The flame inside does not flicker, though the wind is howling through the open door.

The beam sweeps across the room again. The three Fenwicks glance at one another. Oliver closes the door. Esther pours a fourth cup, and they all pretend, with great courtesy, that nothing at all is wrong.
